 ],
 "literal": "Because the rain-place hides, you do not see the sky / Because water spreads and flows, you do not see the ground / Because the sun has gone, darkness has greatly fallen / In the midnight when many sleep / How did you come, O lord of the lofty mountain? / Our small hamlet smells of the Vēṅkai tree / How did you know? I suffer indeed.",
 "translation": "Because the heavy rain hides it, you cannot see the sky. Because water spreads and flows everywhere, you cannot see the ground. Since the sun has set, deep darkness has fallen. In the dead of night when everyone else is asleep, how did you come here, O lord of the lofty mountain? How did you find our small hamlet fragrant with Vēṅkai blossoms? I tremble with worry for you.",
 "modern": "மழை மறைப்பதால் உம்மால் வானத்தைப் பார்க்க முடியாது. நீர் பரவி ஓடுவதால் உம்மால் நிலத்தைப் பார்க்க முடியாது. சூரியன் மறைந்ததால் இருள் பெரிதும் சூழ்ந்துள்ளது. எல்லாரும் உறங்கும் நள்ளிரவில், உயர்ந்த மலையின் தலைவனே, நீர் எவ்வாறு வந்தீர்? வேங்கை மலர்கள் மணக்கும் எங்களது சிறிய குடிலை எவ்வாறு அறிந்து வந்தீர்? உமது வருகையை நினைத்து நான் வருந்துகிறேன்.",
 "explanation": "The companion speaks to the hero who has arrived for a secret tryst at night. She describes the extreme dangers of his journey through the mountain path during a heavy rainstorm. The sky is hidden by clouds, the ground is flooded, and the darkness is absolute. She wonders how he managed to find their small hamlet in such conditions. She notes that the hamlet is fragrant with blooming Vēṅkai trees, which might have guided him, but expresses her deep anxiety for his safety. This Kuriñci landscape poem uses the perilous night journey to gently urge the hero to seek a formal marriage instead of risking his life.",
